    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 15: Kubernetes Architecture & Core Primitives - Control Plane, Nodes & Pods\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Kubernetes Fundamentals & Cluster Architecture\n- **Key Concepts:** Control Plane (`kube-apiserver`, `etcd`, `kube-scheduler`, `kube-controller-manager`), Worker Nodes (`kubelet`, `kube-proxy`, `containerd`), Pod Anatomy, Namespaces, `kubectl` Imperative vs Declarative\n\n---\n\n## 1. Kubernetes Cluster Architectural Overview\n\nWhile Docker manages container lifecycles on a single host, Kubernetes orchestrates automated deployment, scaling, healing, and traffic routing across fleets of distributed virtual and physical machines:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Client([DevOps Admin / kubectl]) -->|HTTPS Port 6443| API[kube-apiserver]\n    subgraph \"Control Plane (Master Node)\"\n        API <--> ETCD[(etcd: Key-Value State DB<br/>Port 2379)]\n        API <--> SCHED[kube-scheduler]\n        API <--> KCM[kube-controller-manager]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Worker Node 1\"\n        Kubelet1[kubelet agent] <--> API\n        Proxy1[kube-proxy]\n        CRI1[Container Runtime: containerd]\n        Pod1[\"Pod: web (Nginx)\"]\n        Kubelet1 --> CRI1\n        CRI1 --> Pod1\n    end\n    subgraph \"Worker Node 2\"\n        Kubelet2[kubelet agent] <--> API\n        Proxy2[kube-proxy]\n        CRI2[Container Runtime: containerd]\n        Pod2[\"Pod: database (MySQL)\"]\n        Kubelet2 --> CRI2\n        CRI2 --> Pod2\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Components Reference Matrix\n\n| Component | Node Type | Core Responsibility | Key Detail / Port |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`kube-apiserver`** | Control Plane | Central REST API gateway; validates and configures data for all API objects. | Port 6443 (HTTPS) |\n| **`etcd`** | Control Plane | Consistent, highly-available key-value store holding cluster state. | Port 2379 (State DB) |\n| **`kube-scheduler`** | Control Plane | Selects the optimal worker node for newly created pods based on resources. | Filters & Scores Nodes |\n| **`kube-controller-manager`** | Control Plane | Runs core control loops: Node Controller, EndpointSlice, Namespace, Replication. | Enforces Desired State |\n| **`kubelet`** | Worker Node | Primary node agent ensuring containers described in PodSpecs are running and healthy. | Communicates via CRI |\n| **`kube-proxy`** | Worker Node | Maintains host network packet filtering rules (iptables/IPVS) for Kubernetes Services. | Service L4 Proxy |\n| **`Container Runtime`** | Worker Node | Low-level container engine responsible for pulling images and executing containers. | containerd / CRI-O |\n\n---\n\n## 3. What is a Pod? (The Atomic Unit of Scheduling)\n\nIn Kubernetes, containers are never deployed directly on nodes. Instead, containers are wrapped inside **Pods**:\n1. **Atomic Unit:** A Pod represents a single instance of a running process in your cluster. It can hold one container (standard) or multiple tightly coupled containers (Sidecar pattern).\n2. **Shared Localhost:** All containers inside a single Pod share the SAME Network Namespace (same IP address and same Port space) and can communicate over `localhost`.\n3. **Shared Storage:** Containers in a Pod share storage volumes defined at the Pod specification level.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Hands-On Lab: First Pods & Namespaces\n\n```bash\n# 1. Launch first standalone (orphan) pod\nkubectl run web-demo --image=nginx:alpine\n\n# 2. Get list of pods with IP and assigned Worker Node\nkubectl get pods -o wide\n\n# 3. Inspect deep runtime configuration, events, and health\nkubectl describe pod web-demo\n\n# 4. View container stdout/stderr logs\nkubectl logs web-demo\n\n# 5. Execute interactive shell inside pod\nkubectl exec -it web-demo -- sh\n\n# 6. Delete pod\nkubectl delete pod web-demo\n```\n\n### Namespaces Management (Logical Multi-Tenancy)\n```bash\n# List all namespaces in cluster\nkubectl get namespaces\n\n# Create dedicated team namespace\nkubectl create namespace staging\n\n# Run pod inside staging namespace\nkubectl run api-server --image=nginx -n staging\n\n# List pods in staging\nkubectl get pods -n staging\n\n# View pods across ALL namespaces in cluster\nkubectl get pods -A\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Declarative Generation: The Dry-Run Technique\n\nProduction Kubernetes forbids manual imperative commands. Use the `--dry-run=client -o yaml` flag to generate clean declarative manifests:\n\n```bash\n# Generate Pod manifest without touching cluster:\nkubectl run webserver --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml\n\n# Apply the manifest declaratively\nkubectl apply -f pod.yaml\n\n# Delete resource using the manifest\nkubectl delete -f pod.yaml\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: Which Kubernetes component directly reads from and writes to etcd?\n> **Answer:** Only the `kube-apiserver` communicates directly with etcd. All other components (scheduler, controller manager, kubelet) communicate with the API server, which acts as the sole gatekeeper and validator.\n\n### Q2: What is the difference between a Container and a Pod?\n> **Answer:** A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes consisting of one or more containers that share network namespaces (IP address and localhost port space) and storage volumes.\n\n### Q3: What happens if a container process inside a Pod crashes?\n> **Answer:** The `kubelet` on that worker node detects the dead container through the container runtime (CRI), checks the Pod `restartPolicy` (default: `Always`), and immediately restarts or recreates the container on that same node.\n\n### Q4: How do you inspect the documentation and available fields of a Kubernetes YAML object from CLI?\n> **Answer:** Use `kubectl explain <resource>.<field>`, for example `kubectl explain pod.spec.containers`. It prints documentation, descriptions, and field types directly in your terminal.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    Client([DevOps Admin / kubectl]) -->|HTTPS Port 6443| API[kube-apiserver]\n    subgraph \"Control Plane (Master Node)\"\n        API <--> ETCD[(etcd: Key-Value State DB<br/>Port 2379)]\n        API <--> SCHED[kube-scheduler]\n        API <--> KCM[kube-controller-manager]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Worker Node 1\"\n        Kubelet1[kubelet agent] <--> API\n        Proxy1[kube-proxy]\n        CRI1[Container Runtime: containerd]\n        Pod1[\"Pod: web (Nginx)\"]\n        Kubelet1 --> CRI1\n        CRI1 --> Pod1\n    end\n    subgraph \"Worker Node 2\"\n        Kubelet2[kubelet agent] <--> API\n        Proxy2[kube-proxy]\n        CRI2[Container Runtime: containerd]\n        Pod2[\"Pod: database (MySQL)\"]\n        Kubelet2 --> CRI2\n        CRI2 --> Pod2\n    end",
    "commands": "# BASH\n# 1. Launch first standalone (orphan) pod\nkubectl run web-demo --image=nginx:alpine\n\n# 2. Get list of pods with IP and assigned Worker Node\nkubectl get pods -o wide\n\n# 3. Inspect deep runtime configuration, events, and health\nkubectl describe pod web-demo\n\n# 4. View container stdout/stderr logs\nkubectl logs web-demo\n\n# 5. Execute interactive shell inside pod\nkubectl exec -it web-demo -- sh\n\n# 6. Delete pod\nkubectl delete pod web-demo\n\n# BASH\n# List all namespaces in cluster\nkubectl get namespaces\n\n# Create dedicated team namespace\nkubectl create namespace staging\n\n# Run pod inside staging namespace\nkubectl run api-server --image=nginx -n staging\n\n# List pods in staging\nkubectl get pods -n staging\n\n# View pods across ALL namespaces in cluster\nkubectl get pods -A\n\n# BASH\n# Generate Pod manifest without touching cluster:\nkubectl run webserver --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml\n\n# Apply the manifest declaratively\nkubectl apply -f pod.yaml\n\n# Delete resource using the manifest\nkubectl delete -f pod.yaml",
