    "icon": "dharmachakra",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 16: Kubernetes Controllers - ReplicationController, ReplicaSet & Selectors\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Pod Lifecycle Management & Reconciliation Controllers\n- **Key Concepts:** Orphan Pods vs Managed Pods, ReplicationController (RC) vs ReplicaSet (RS), Equality vs Set-Based Selectors, Scale-In/Scale-Out, Self-Healing\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Orphan Pod Problem & Self-Healing Architecture\n\nPods created directly via `kubectl run` are known as **Orphan Pods**. If their host worker node crashes or runs out of memory, these pods are permanently terminated and never rescheduled. Controllers enforce desired state and high availability:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    User([Kubernetes Admin]) -->|Apply Replicas: 3| RS[ReplicaSet Controller]\n    subgraph \"Desired State Loop (Reconciliation)\"\n        RS -->|Watch via Labels app=web| P1[\"Pod 1 (Running)\"]\n        RS -->|Watch via Labels app=web| P2[\"Pod 2 (Running)\"]\n        RS -->|Pod 3 Crashes!| P3[\"Pod 3 (DEAD)\"]\n        P3 -.->|Detects Desired:3, Actual:2| RS\n        RS -->|Immediately Spawns New Pod| P4[\"Pod 4 (NEW Running)\"]\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. ReplicationController (RC) vs ReplicaSet (RS) Matrix\n\n| Criteria | ReplicationController (v1) | ReplicaSet (apps/v1) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **API Version** | `core/v1` | `apps/v1` |\n| **Selector Types** | Equality-based ONLY (`app: nginx`) | Equality-based AND Set-based (`matchExpressions`) |\n| **Industry Adoption** | Deprecated / Legacy | Standard controller powering Deployments |\n| **Adoption Flexibility** | Matches exact key-value pairs | Can match complex environments (e.g. `tier in (frontend, qa)`) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Labels & Selectors Masterclass\n\nLabels are key-value pairs attached to objects. Selectors are queries used by controllers and services to group related pods:\n\n```bash\n# Query pods by label\nkubectl get pods --show-labels\nkubectl get pods -l tier=frontend\nkubectl get pods -l 'env in (production, staging)'\n\n# Add or overwrite labels dynamically\nkubectl label pod mypod tier=backend --overwrite\n\n# Delete pods matching a specific label\nkubectl delete pod -l tier=frontend\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Production ReplicaSet Manifest Blueprint\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: ReplicaSet\nmetadata:\n  name: frontend-scaler\n  labels:\n    app: guestbook\n    tier: frontend\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchExpressions:\n      - {key: tier, operator: In, values: [frontend]}\n      - {key: environment, operator: NotIn, values: [dev]}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        tier: frontend\n        environment: prod\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: webapp\n        image: nginx:alpine\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Dynamic Scaling Operations\n\n```bash\n# Scale up: Increase replicas to 5\nkubectl scale --replicas=5 rs frontend-scaler\n\n# Scale down: Reduce replicas to 1\nkubectl scale --replicas=1 rs frontend-scaler\n\n# Scale to 0: Destroys all pods while preserving the controller object\nkubectl scale --replicas=0 rs frontend-scaler\n\n# Verification\nkubectl get rs\nkubectl get pods -o wide\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is an Orphan Pod in Kubernetes, and why should it be avoided in production?\n> **Answer:** An orphan pod is not managed by any controller. If the pod process exits or the node dies, Kubernetes will never recreate it. Controllers maintain the desired replica count and ensure automatic self-healing.\n\n### Q2: What is the key technological advancement in ReplicaSet over ReplicationController?\n> **Answer:** ReplicationController only supports equality-based matching (`key=value`). ReplicaSet additionally supports set-based matching using `matchExpressions` with operators like `In`, `NotIn`, and `Exists`.\n\n### Q3: If a ReplicaSet has `replicas: 3` and you manually create a 4th pod with the exact same labels, what happens?\n> **Answer:** The ReplicaSet detects 4 actual pods matching its selector while the desired count is 3. It will immediately terminate one pod to maintain the desired count of 3.\n\n### Q4: What happens when you execute `kubectl scale --replicas=0 rs/<name>`?\n> **Answer:** All running pods belonging to the ReplicaSet are terminated and removed, but the ReplicaSet object itself remains active in etcd with 0 instances, ready to be scaled back up at any time.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    User([Kubernetes Admin]) -->|Apply Replicas: 3| RS[ReplicaSet Controller]\n    subgraph \"Desired State Loop (Reconciliation)\"\n        RS -->|Watch via Labels app=web| P1[\"Pod 1 (Running)\"]\n        RS -->|Watch via Labels app=web| P2[\"Pod 2 (Running)\"]\n        RS -->|Pod 3 Crashes!| P3[\"Pod 3 (DEAD)\"]\n        P3 -.->|Detects Desired:3, Actual:2| RS\n        RS -->|Immediately Spawns New Pod| P4[\"Pod 4 (NEW Running)\"]\n    end",
    "commands": "# YAML\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: ReplicaSet\nmetadata:\n  name: frontend-scaler\n  labels:\n    app: guestbook\n    tier: frontend\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchExpressions:\n      - {key: tier, operator: In, values: [frontend]}\n      - {key: environment, operator: NotIn, values: [dev]}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        tier: frontend\n        environment: prod\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: webapp\n        image: nginx:alpine\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80",
