    "color": "#326CE5",
    "icon": "dharmachakra",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 17: Kubernetes Deployments - RollingUpdate, Recreate & Rollbacks\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Kubernetes Deployment Controllers & Release Engineering\n- **Key Concepts:** How Deployments Manage ReplicaSets, Zero-Downtime Updates, `RollingUpdate` vs `Recreate`, `maxSurge` & `maxUnavailable`, Revisions & Automated Rollbacks\n\n---\n\n## 1. How Deployments Manage ReplicaSets (Zero-Downtime Architecture)\n\nDeployments do not manage pods directly. A Deployment creates and manages underlying ReplicaSets. During a version update, a new ReplicaSet is spun up while the previous ReplicaSet is scaled down incrementally:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Deploy[\"Deployment: web-app (Version 2)\"] --> RS2[\"New ReplicaSet: web-app-v2<br/>(Scaling UP: 1 -> 2 -> 3)\"]\n    Deploy --> RS1[\"Old ReplicaSet: web-app-v1<br/>(Scaling DOWN: 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 0)\"]\n    RS2 --> P4[\"Pod v2\"]\n    RS2 --> P5[\"Pod v2\"]\n    RS1 -.->|Graceful Termination| P1[\"Pod v1\"]\n    RS1 -.->|Graceful Termination| P2[\"Pod v1\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Deployment Strategies: RollingUpdate vs Recreate\n\n| Strategy | Downtime? | Resource Overhead | Rollout Behavior | Best Used For |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`RollingUpdate` (Default)** | 0% (Zero Downtime) | Requires extra capacity (`maxSurge`) | Gradually replaces old pods with new pods. | Web applications, microservices, stateless APIs. |\n| **`Recreate`** | Yes (Temporary Outage) | None (Old pods killed first) | Terminates ALL running pods before launching new ones. | Legacy apps, breaking database migrations, single-writer files. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production Deployment YAML Blueprint\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-production\n  labels:\n    app: nginx\nspec:\n  replicas: 4\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      maxSurge: 25%          # Max 1 extra pod launched during update (4 * 0.25 = 1)\n      maxUnavailable: 25%    # Max 1 pod can be offline during update\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx:1.20-alpine\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Rollout & Rollback Lifecycle Commands\n\n```bash\n# Update application image with revision recording\nkubectl set image deploy/nginx-production nginx=nginx:1.21-alpine --record\n\n# Monitor rollout progress in real-time\nkubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-production\n\n# View historical deployment revisions\nkubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-production\n\n# View details of a specific revision\nkubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-production --revision=2\n\n# Instant Rollback: Revert to previous revision\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-production\n\n# Rollback to a specific historical revision\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-production --to-revision=1\n\n# Pause rollout (useful for canary testing a subset of pods)\nkubectl rollout pause deployment/nginx-production\n\n# Resume rollout to complete the update across all pods\nkubectl rollout resume deployment/nginx-production\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the difference between a ReplicaSet and a Deployment?\n> **Answer:** A ReplicaSet manages a specific set of identical pods. A Deployment manages ReplicaSets, enabling declarative rollouts, zero-downtime rolling updates, and rollbacks.\n\n### Q2: Explain maxSurge and maxUnavailable in RollingUpdate strategy.\n> **Answer:** `maxSurge` defines the maximum number of pods that can be created above the desired replica count during an update. `maxUnavailable` defines the maximum number of pods that can be unavailable during the update process.\n\n### Q3: How do you roll back a failed deployment to a specific stable revision?\n> **Answer:** Use `kubectl rollout undo deploy/<deployment_name> --to-revision=<number>`. Kubernetes immediately scales up the target revision's ReplicaSet and scales down the broken one.\n\n### Q4: Under what scenario would you choose `Recreate` strategy over `RollingUpdate`?\n> **Answer:** Use the `Recreate` strategy when application versions cannot run concurrently, such as when a breaking database schema migration or exclusive storage lock prevents backwards compatibility.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    Deploy[\"Deployment: web-app (Version 2)\"] --> RS2[\"New ReplicaSet: web-app-v2<br/>(Scaling UP: 1 -> 2 -> 3)\"]\n    Deploy --> RS1[\"Old ReplicaSet: web-app-v1<br/>(Scaling DOWN: 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 0)\"]\n    RS2 --> P4[\"Pod v2\"]\n    RS2 --> P5[\"Pod v2\"]\n    RS1 -.->|Graceful Termination| P1[\"Pod v1\"]\n    RS1 -.->|Graceful Termination| P2[\"Pod v1\"]",
    "commands": "# YAML\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-production\n  labels:\n    app: nginx\nspec:\n  replicas: 4\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      maxSurge: 25%          # Max 1 extra pod launched during update (4 * 0.25 = 1)\n      maxUnavailable: 25%    # Max 1 pod can be offline during update\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx:1.20-alpine\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80",
