      {
        "q": "Why is there no 'User' resource in Kubernetes (e.g. 'kubectl get users' does not exist)?",
        "a": "Kubernetes does not store human users in etcd. Users are authenticated externally via signed X.509 client certificates or external Identity Providers (OIDC/SAML/LDAP)."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is the difference between a Normal User and a ServiceAccount?",
        "a": "ServiceAccounts are designed for in-cluster processes/pods and are stored as API resources in etcd. Normal users represent humans outside the cluster and cannot be managed via `kubectl create user`."
      },
      {
        "q": "What happens when you bind a 'ClusterRole' using a 'RoleBinding' instead of a 'ClusterRoleBinding'?",
        "a": "When a ClusterRole is bound using a RoleBinding, the permissions defined in the ClusterRole are restricted strictly to the namespace of that RoleBinding. This allows reusing generic roles (like `admin` or `view`) across hundreds of namespaces without duplicating role definitions."
      },
      {
        "q": "How can an administrator safely test whether a user or ServiceAccount has permission to perform an action?",
        "a": "The `kubectl auth can-i` command queries the Kubernetes API server SelfSubjectAccessReview / SubjectAccessReview API to evaluate RBAC rules without executing the actual mutation."
      }
