    "moduleBadge": "Security & CNI",
    "color": "#EC4899",
    "icon": "shield-halved",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 21: Kubernetes Pod Networking & Core Services - Calico CNI, ClusterIP & NodePort\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Kubernetes Networking & Service Discovery\n- **Key Concepts:** Kubernetes Networking Model, Calico CNI & IPAM (`blockSize`, CIDR pools), The Ephemeral IP Problem, `kube-proxy` Data Plane (iptables/IPVS), `ClusterIP`, `NodePort` (30000-32767), and Production 2-Tier Architecture (WordPress + MySQL)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Kubernetes Networking Axioms & CNI Architecture\n\nKubernetes requires:\n1. **Flat Address Space:** All Pods can communicate with all other Pods without NAT.\n2. **Node-to-Pod Parity:** Agents on a node can reach all local Pods directly.\n3. **IP Identity Consistency:** A Pod sees itself using the same IP that the rest of the cluster sees.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Calico CNI: Routing, IPAM & BGP Mechanics\n\nProject Calico implements pure Layer 3 routing using Linux kernel routing tables and BGP (Border Gateway Protocol).\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    subgraph Node1[\"Worker Node 1 (192.168.1.10)\"]\n        P1[\"Pod 1<br/>10.244.1.2\"] --- V1[\"veth1\"]\n        V1 --- R1[\"Linux Route Table\"]\n        BGP1[\"Calico Felix / BGP Agent\"]\n    end\n    subgraph Node2[\"Worker Node 2 (192.168.1.20)\"]\n        P2[\"Pod 2<br/>10.244.2.5\"] --- V2[\"veth2\"]\n        V2 --- R2[\"Linux Route Table\"]\n        BGP2[\"Calico Felix / BGP Agent\"]\n    end\n    R1 <==>|Direct L3 BGP or IP-in-IP Tunnel| R2\n    BGP1 <==>|BGP Route Exchange| BGP2\n```\n\nCalico Subnet Pool Configuration:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: projectcalico.org/v3\nkind: IPPool\nmetadata:\n  name: production-ippool\nspec:\n  cidr: 192.168.0.0/16       # Cluster-wide pod CIDR\n  blockSize: 26              # Allocates /26 (64 IPs) per worker node\n  ipipMode: Always           # Encapsulates cross-subnet traffic via IP-in-IP\n  natOutgoing: true          # Outbound internet traffic uses node NAT\n  nodeSelector: all()\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why Services? The Ephemeral IP Problem\n\nPods are temporary and disposable. When pods terminate or scale, their IP addresses vanish. A Service provides an immutable virtual IP (`ClusterIP`) and stable DNS record.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Client[\"Client / Frontend Pod\"] -->|Sends to Virtual VIP| SVC[\"Kubernetes Service: order-svc<br/>ClusterIP: 10.96.120.45:80\"]\n    SVC --> KP[\"kube-proxy (iptables / IPVS Load Balancing)\"]\n    KP --> EP1[\"Backend Pod 1 (10.244.1.15:8080)\"]\n    KP --> EP2[\"Backend Pod 2 (10.244.2.22:8080)\"]\n    KP --> EP3[\"Backend Pod 3 (10.244.1.19:8080)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Service Port Mapping Decoded\n\n| Port Directive | Location | Purpose / Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`port`** | Service Virtual IP (VIP) | The port exposed internally by the Service itself. |\n| **`targetPort`** | Container inside Pod | The port on which the container application actually listens. |\n| **`nodePort`** | Physical Worker Node IP | The cluster-wide external port opened on every worker node (30000-32767). |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Service Type 1: `ClusterIP` (Internal Microservices)\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: mysql-db-svc\n  namespace: default\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  selector:\n    app: mysql               # Selects pods with label 'app: mysql'\n  ports:\n  - name: mysql-port\n    protocol: TCP\n    port: 3306               # Service Port\n    targetPort: 3306         # Container Port\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Service Type 2: `NodePort` (External Inbound Access)\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: frontend-nodeport-svc\n  namespace: default\nspec:\n  type: NodePort\n  selector:\n    app: web-frontend\n  ports:\n  - name: http\n    protocol: TCP\n    port: 80                 # Service Port\n    targetPort: 80           # Container Port\n    nodePort: 30080          # Accessible at http://<Worker-IP>:30080\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Full Production Lab: 2-Tier WordPress & MySQL Deployment\n\n```yaml\n# MySQL Backend Deployment & Service\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: mysql\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: mysql\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: mysql\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: mysql\n        image: mysql:5.7\n        env:\n        - name: MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD\n          value: \"RootPassword@123\"\n        - name: MYSQL_DATABASE\n          value: \"wordpress_db\"\n        - name: MYSQL_USER\n          value: \"wpuser\"\n        - name: MYSQL_PASSWORD\n          value: \"WpPass@123\"\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 3306\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: mysql-service\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  selector:\n    app: mysql\n  ports:\n  - port: 3306\n    targetPort: 3306\n---\n# WordPress Frontend Deployment & NodePort Service\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: wordpress\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: wordpress\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: wordpress\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: wordpress\n        image: wordpress:latest\n        env:\n        - name: WORDPRESS_DB_HOST\n          value: \"mysql-service.default.svc.cluster.local:3306\"\n        - name: WORDPRESS_DB_NAME\n          value: \"wordpress_db\"\n        - name: WORDPRESS_DB_USER\n          value: \"wpuser\"\n        - name: WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD\n          value: \"WpPass@123\"\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: wordpress-ext-svc\nspec:\n  type: NodePort\n  selector:\n    app: wordpress\n  ports:\n  - port: 80\n    targetPort: 80\n    nodePort: 30088\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Verification & Diagnostics\n\n```bash\n# Check all running services\nkubectl get svc -o wide\n\n# Check underlying Endpoints linked to services\nkubectl get endpoints\nkubectl describe service wordpress-ext-svc\n\n# Verify connectivity from inside a pod\nkubectl run test-pod --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it -- nslookup mysql-service\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfaf Day 21 Review & Interview Questions\n\n1. **Why do we need a Kubernetes Service if every Pod already has its own IP address?**\n   - Pod IPs are ephemeral; they change every time a pod crashes, restarts, or scales. A Service provides a permanent, immutable virtual IP (VIP) and stable DNS name, backed by automatic load balancing across healthy pods.\n2. **How does kube-proxy handle traffic routing without causing a performance bottleneck?**\n   - `kube-proxy` does NOT sit in the data path as a proxy server. Instead, it monitors the API server for changes to Services and Endpoints, and writes Linux kernel packet filtering rules (using `iptables` or `IPVS`) to perform Destination NAT (DNAT) directly in kernel space.\n3. **What happens if 'targetPort' is omitted in a Service definition?**\n   - If `targetPort` is not specified, Kubernetes defaults `targetPort` to be identical to the value specified in the `port` field.\n4. **What is the difference between IP-in-IP and VXLAN encapsulation in Calico?**\n   - IP-in-IP encapsulates an IP packet inside another IP packet (Layer 3 over Layer 3), whereas VXLAN encapsulates Layer 2 Ethernet frames inside Layer 4 UDP packets (port 4789). Both allow cross-subnet pod communication.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph LR\n    subgraph Node1[\"Worker Node 1 (192.168.1.10)\"]\n        P1[\"Pod 1<br/>10.244.1.2\"] --- V1[\"veth1\"]\n        V1 --- R1[\"Linux Route Table\"]\n        BGP1[\"Calico Felix / BGP Agent\"]\n    end\n    subgraph Node2[\"Worker Node 2 (192.168.1.20)\"]\n        P2[\"Pod 2<br/>10.244.2.5\"] --- V2[\"veth2\"]\n        V2 --- R2[\"Linux Route Table\"]\n        BGP2[\"Calico Felix / BGP Agent\"]\n    end\n    R1 <==>|Direct L3 BGP or IP-in-IP Tunnel| R2\n    BGP1 <==>|BGP Route Exchange| BGP2",
