    "icon": "chart-line",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 25: Kubernetes DaemonSets, Health Probes & Helm Package Management (with Jenkins)\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Node-Level Workloads, Autonomous Self-Healing & Package Management\n- **Key Concepts:** `DaemonSets` (Architecture, Taint Tolerations, Log Shipping), Health Probes (`livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, `startupProbe`, `httpGet`, `tcpSocket`, `exec`), `Helm 3` Architecture (Charts, `values.yaml`, Releases, History & Rollbacks), and Production Deployment of Jenkins CI/CD via Helm\n\n---\n\n## 1. DaemonSet: Node-Level Service Architecture\n\nA DaemonSet guarantees that all (or a selected subset of) worker nodes run exactly one copy of a Pod.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    DS[\"DaemonSet Controller: fluentd-logging\"] --> Node1[\"Worker Node 1\"]\n    DS --> Node2[\"Worker Node 2\"]\n    DS --> Node3[\"Worker Node 3 (Master / Control Plane)\"]\n    Node1 --> P1[\"Fluentd Pod 1<br/>(Collecting Node 1 Logs)\"]\n    Node2 --> P2[\"Fluentd Pod 2<br/>(Collecting Node 2 Logs)\"]\n    Node3 --> P3[\"Fluentd Pod 3<br/>(Requires Toleration for Master Taint)\"]\n```\n\nProduction DaemonSet YAML Blueprint:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: DaemonSet\nmetadata:\n  name: fluentd-elasticsearch\n  namespace: kube-logging\n  labels:\n    app: fluentd\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: fluentd\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: fluentd\n    spec:\n      serviceAccountName: fluentd-sa\n      # Tolerate control plane taint to run on master nodes:\n      tolerations:\n      - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane\n        operator: Exists\n        effect: NoSchedule\n      containers:\n      - name: fluentd\n        image: fluent/fluentd-kubernetes-daemonset:v1.4.2\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 500Mi\n          requests:\n            cpu: 100m\n            memory: 200Mi\n        volumeMounts:\n        - name: varlog\n          mountPath: /var/log\n      volumes:\n      - name: varlog\n        hostPath:\n          path: /var/log\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Container Self-Healing: Health Probes\n\n| Probe Type | Purpose / Evaluation | Action On Repeated Failure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`livenessProbe`** | Detects if container is deadlocked or broken. | Kubelet KILLS and RESTARTS container according to `restartPolicy`. |\n| **`readinessProbe`**| Detects if container is ready to serve network traffic. | Pod IP is REMOVED from Service Endpoints; container is NOT killed. |\n| **`startupProbe`**  | Protects slow-starting applications during initial boot. | Disables liveness and readiness checks until it succeeds. |\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: hardened-web-api\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: web-api\n    image: my-enterprise-api:v2\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 8080\n    startupProbe:\n      httpGet:\n        path: /healthz\n        port: 8080\n      failureThreshold: 30\n      periodSeconds: 10\n    livenessProbe:\n      httpGet:\n        path: /live\n        port: 8080\n      initialDelaySeconds: 5\n      periodSeconds: 10\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3\n    readinessProbe:\n      httpGet:\n        path: /ready\n        port: 8080\n      initialDelaySeconds: 5\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      successThreshold: 1\n      failureThreshold: 2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Helm 3: The Kubernetes Package Manager\n\nHelm 3 operates strictly as a client-side CLI using RBAC credentials from `kubeconfig`:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    Dev[\"DevOps Engineer\"] -->|helm install / upgrade| CLI[\"Helm 3 CLI\"]\n    CLI -->|Evaluates templates + values.yaml| K8sAPI[\"kube-apiserver\"]\n    K8sAPI -->|Stores Release State in Secrets| K8sSecret[(\"Helm Release Secret<br/>sh.helm.release.v1.app.v1\")]\n    K8sAPI --> Workloads[\"Deploys Deployments, Services, Ingress, PVCs\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Production Helm Workflow & Commands\n\n```bash\n# Repository Management\nhelm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo bitnami/nginx\n\n# Chart Installation & Customization\nhelm install prod-web bitnami/nginx -n production   --set replicaCount=3   --set service.type=LoadBalancer\n\n# Inspecting Releases & History\nhelm list -n production\nhelm history prod-web -n production\n\n# Upgrading & Rolling Back\nhelm upgrade prod-web bitnami/nginx -n production --set replicaCount=5\nhelm rollback prod-web 1 -n production\n\n# Deleting a Release completely\nhelm uninstall prod-web -n production\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Real-World Lab: Deploying Jenkins CI/CD via Helm\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Add official Jenkins Helm repository\nhelm repo add jenkins https://charts.jenkins.io\nhelm repo update\n\n# Step 2: Create namespace\nkubectl create namespace devops\n\n# Step 3: Deploy Jenkins with customized persistence and NodePort\nhelm install jenkins-server jenkins/jenkins -n devops   --set controller.serviceType=NodePort   --set controller.nodePort=32000   --set persistence.size=10Gi\n\n# Step 4: Retrieve auto-generated Jenkins Admin Password\nkubectl exec --namespace devops -it svc/jenkins-server -c jenkins   -- /bin/cat /run/secrets/additional/chart-admin-password && echo\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfaf Day 25 Review & Interview Questions\n\n1. **Why does a failing readinessProbe NOT restart the container like a livenessProbe does?**\n   - A failing livenessProbe kills and restarts the container to recover from deadlocks. A failing readinessProbe simply removes the pod from service endpoints so it stops receiving user traffic, giving it time to recover without destroying its memory or state.\n2. **How does a DaemonSet pod get scheduled onto a master node that has a 'NoSchedule' taint?**\n   - Adding a toleration for `node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule` in the DaemonSet pod spec allows the DaemonSet controller to place pods on control plane nodes.\n3. **What was the most significant architectural change between Helm 2 and Helm 3?**\n   - In Helm 2, an in-cluster server component called 'Tiller' managed releases with cluster-admin privileges, posing severe security risks. Helm 3 completely removed Tiller, adopting a direct client-only model that inherits the operator's kubeconfig RBAC permissions and stores release states inside Kubernetes Secrets.\n4. **What is the purpose of a 'startupProbe' and when should it be used?**\n   - A 'startupProbe' disables liveness and readiness probes until the container successfully passes startup checks. This gives slow-booting applications (like Java Enterprise apps) plenty of time to warm up without being prematurely killed by an aggressive livenessProbe.\n",
