    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 26: Advanced Pod Patterns, Deployment Strategies & Observability: Static Pods, Sidecars, Init Containers, Blue/Green, Canary, HPA & Prometheus\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Advanced Pod Patterns, Zero-Downtime Rollouts & Observability\n- **Key Concepts:** Multi-Container Patterns (Sidecar, Adapter, Ambassador), `Init Containers`, `Static Pods` (`/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`, Kubelet direct management), Deployment Strategies (`Blue/Green` vs `Canary`), Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (`HPA`), and Cluster Monitoring with `Prometheus` & `Grafana`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Multi-Container Pod Design Patterns\n\nContainers inside the same Pod share the network namespace (`localhost:127.0.0.1`), IPC, and storage:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    subgraph Pod[\"Single Pod (IP: 10.244.1.42)\"]\n        Main[\"Main Application Container<br/>(Node.js / Java API)\"]\n        Sidecar[\"Sidecar Container<br/>(Log Shipper / Envoy Proxy)\"]\n        Vol[(\"Shared emptyDir Volume<br/>/var/log/app\")]\n        Main -->|Writes Logs| Vol\n        Sidecar -->|Reads & Streams Logs| Vol\n        Main <-->|Localhost IPC / Network| Sidecar\n    end\n```\n\n- **Sidecar:** Extends main container (e.g. log shipping, proxying).\n- **Adapter:** Normalizes outputs to standard formats (e.g. Prometheus metrics exporter).\n- **Ambassador:** Proxies local connections to external clusters (e.g. Redis sentinel).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Init Containers: Pre-Flight Execution\n\nInit containers run sequentially to completion before any application containers start:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: secured-es-pod\nspec:\n  initContainers:\n  - name: init-sysctl\n    image: busybox:latest\n    securityContext:\n      privileged: true       # Elevated privilege granted ONLY to setup container\n    command: [\"sysctl\", \"-w\", \"vm.max_map_count=262144\"]\n  - name: wait-for-db\n    command: ['sh', '-c', 'until nc -z -v -w3 mysql-service 3306; do echo waiting for db; sleep 2; done;']\n  containers:\n  - name: web-app\n    image: my-enterprise-app:v1\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 8080\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Static Pods: Direct Kubelet Workloads\n\nStatic Pods are managed directly by `kubelet` on a specific node without the API server:\n- **Location:** `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`\n- **Control Plane Bootstrapping:** `etcd`, `kube-apiserver`, `kube-controller-manager`, and `kube-scheduler` run as Static Pods!\n\n```bash\n# Inspect static pod manifests on control plane node\nls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/\n\n# View running static pods (note node suffix)\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Production Release Strategies: Blue/Green vs Canary\n\n| Release Strategy | Traffic Transition Mechanism | Resource Overhead | Rollback Speed | Target Use Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`RollingUpdate`** | Pod-by-pod replacement. | Low (+25% surge). | Slow (requires rollout). | Standard web apps. |\n| **`Blue/Green`** | Instant traffic cutover via Service selector (`version: green`). | High (200% capacity). | Instantaneous (re-patch service). | Mission-critical apps, schema changes. |\n| **`Canary`** | Routing 5-10% of traffic to new release pods. | Low (1-2 canary replicas). | Fast (scale canary to 0). | High-risk feature verification. |\n\n```bash\n# Execute instant Blue/Green cutover\nkubectl patch service production-web-svc -p '{\"spec\":{\"selector\":{\"version\":\"green\"}}}'\n\n# Instant 1-second Rollback\nkubectl patch service production-web-svc -p '{\"spec\":{\"selector\":{\"version\":\"blue\"}}}'\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: dynamic-web-hpa\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: web-api\n  minReplicas: 2\n  maxReplicas: 20\n  metrics:\n  - type: Resource\n    resource:\n      name: cpu\n      target:\n        type: Utilization\n        averageUtilization: 75\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Observability: Prometheus & Grafana Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    Pods[\"Target Pods & Nodes<br/>(/metrics HTTP endpoint)\"] -->|Scraped by HTTP Pull| Prom[\"Prometheus Server<br/>(Time-Series Database)\"]\n    Prom --> Alert[\"Alertmanager<br/>(PagerDuty / Slack)\"]\n    Grafana[\"Grafana Dashboards\"] -->|PromQL Queries| Prom\n```\n\n```bash\n# Deploy Prometheus and Grafana stack using Helm\nhelm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts\nhelm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts\nhelm repo update\n\nhelm install prometheus prometheus-community/prometheus -n monitoring --create-namespace\nhelm install grafana grafana/grafana -n monitoring\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfaf Day 26 Review & Interview Questions\n\n1. **How do you identify a Static Pod from the output of 'kubectl get pods'?**\n   - Static Pod names always have the node hostname appended as a suffix (e.g. `kube-apiserver-master-node1`). Furthermore, they cannot be modified, scaled, or deleted via kubectl; their ownerReference points to Node.\n2. **What happens if an administrator runs 'kubectl delete pod <static-pod-name>'?**\n   - The API server will temporarily show the pod terminating, but the local kubelet on that node will detect the missing container and immediately recreate and restart it from its manifest in `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`.\n3. **How do you permanently delete a Static Pod?**\n   - To permanently remove a Static Pod, you must SSH into the node hosting it and delete or move the YAML manifest file out of the `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/` directory.\n4. **What is the difference between Blue/Green and Canary deployment strategies?**\n   - In Blue/Green, traffic is flipped 100% all at once from Blue to Green. In Canary, a small fraction of users (e.g. 5-10%) are directed to the new release while the remaining 90-95% continue on the old release, allowing real-world verification before full rollout.\n",
